# 4. Replicat parameter file

Example filename:

```text
REPTRDS3.prm
```

Configuration:

```text
REPLICAT REPTRDS3

TARGETDB LIBFILE libggjava.so
SET property=/u01/app/ogg/etc/conf/ogg/REPTRDS3.properties

-- Conservative initial value; tune after load testing
GROUPTRANSOPS 5000

MAP TRADE.EXECUTIONS, TARGET TRADE.EXECUTIONS;
MAP TRADE.ORDERS, TARGET TRADE.ORDERS;
MAP TRADE.TRADES, TARGET TRADE.TRADES;
MAP TRADE.FILLS, TARGET TRADE.FILLS;
MAP TRADE.POSITIONS, TARGET TRADE.POSITIONS;
MAP TRADE.SETTLEMENTS, TARGET TRADE.SETTLEMENTS;

-- Add the remaining selected tables here.
```

A wildcard mapping can be used when every table in the schema is intentionally included:

```text
MAP TRADE.*, TARGET TRADE.*;
```

Explicit mappings are safer when the ingestion scope contains only a selected set of tables.

`TARGETDB LIBFILE libggjava.so` loads GoldenGate Java Delivery, while `SET property` points to the handler properties file.

Oracle documents a default `GROUPTRANSOPS` value of 1,000 and supports increasing it toward 20,000 for throughput. Higher values can increase JVM memory use, so 5,000 is a conservative project starting point rather than an Oracle default.

Start with one Classic Replicat for the 50 tables. Oracle recommends establishing a Classic Replicat baseline before moving to Coordinated Replicat. Split the workload or introduce coordination only when measured lag, throughput or failure-isolation requirements justify it.

