## 5.7 S3 Event Handler

```properties
# ============================================================
# S3 EVENT HANDLER
# ============================================================

gg.eventhandler.s3.type=s3

# Required even when using a private AWS endpoint.
gg.eventhandler.s3.region=<aws-region>

gg.eventhandler.s3.bucketMappingTemplate=<raw-cdc-bucket>

gg.eventhandler.s3.pathMappingTemplate=\
oracle-cdc/\
dptf_source_system=oracle_trade/\
dptf_source_database=trading_prod/\
dptf_source_schema=${schemaName}/\
dptf_source_table=${tableName}

# Do not request bucket creation/admin permissions.
gg.eventhandler.s3.enableBucketAdmin=false

# Keep the uploaded S3 object.
gg.eventhandler.s3.finalizeAction=none

# Disable destructive startup behaviour explicitly.
gg.eventhandler.s3.purgeTargetPathOnStartup=false

# 256 MB files do not require multipart upload.
# Keep disabled initially to reduce memory/concurrency complexity.
gg.eventhandler.s3.multipartEnabled=false
```

Oracle’s S3 Handler properties include region, bucket mapping, path mapping, file naming, private endpoint configuration, encryption and credential options.

### Private AWS S3 endpoint or S3-compatible storage

For a private AWS endpoint or an S3-compatible platform:

```properties
gg.eventhandler.s3.url=https://<private-s3-endpoint>
```

Oracle documents `url` for Amazon S3 private endpoints and S3-compatible services such as Dell ECS.

The endpoint, certificate trust and bucket-addressing behaviour must be validated against the actual private storage implementation.

### Credentials

Preferred on AWS:

```properties
# Leave both unset and use the AWS default credentials provider chain.

# gg.eventhandler.s3.accessKeyId=
# gg.eventhandler.s3.secretKey=
```

Oracle documents that when explicit credentials are absent, the S3 Handler falls back to the AWS default credentials provider chain.

For explicit credentials:

```properties
gg.eventhandler.s3.accessKeyId=<wallet-resolved-access-key>
gg.eventhandler.s3.secretKey=<wallet-resolved-secret>
```

Do not commit clear-text secrets to source control.

### Optional server-side encryption

For AWS S3 with KMS:

```properties
gg.eventhandler.s3.SSEAlgorithm=aws:kms
gg.eventhandler.s3.AWSKmsKeyId=<kms-key-or-alias>
```

For S3-managed AES-256:

```properties
gg.eventhandler.s3.SSEAlgorithm=AES256
```

Oracle documents both encryption modes and the KMS key property.

